{"page": 21, "text": "The Company owns a 40% equity investment in Chelsea Investment Holding Company PTE Ltd., which is majority owned by Ctrip Investment Holding Ltd, a majority-owned subsidiary of Trip.com Group Limited. The Company accounts for this minority investment under the equity method, given it has the ability to exercise significant influence, but not control, over the investee. The carrying value of this minority investment was $25.0 million and $25.9 million as of September 30, 2026 and December 31, 2025,\n17\nrespectively, and is included in non-marketable investments on our unaudited condensed consolidated balance sheets. During the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, we recognized $0.2 million and $0.9 million, respectively, representing our share of the investee\u2019s net loss in other income (expenses), net within the unaudited condensed consolidated statements of operations. During the three and nine months ended September 30, 2025, we recognized $0.7 million and $1.6 million, respectively, representing our share of the investee\u2019s net loss in other income (expenses), net within the unaudited condensed consolidated statements of operations. The Company evaluates this investment for impairment when factors indicate that a decline in the value of its investment has occurred and the carrying amount of its investment may not be recoverable. An impairment loss, based on the excess of the carrying value over the estimated fair value of the investment based on Level 3 inputs, is recognized in earnings when an impairment is deemed to be other than temporary. During the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025, we did not record any impairment loss on this equity investment.\nThe Company maintains various commercial agreements with Chelsea Investment Holding Company PTE Ltd. and/or its subsidiaries. Transactions under these agreements are considered related party transactions, and were not material during the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025.\nOther non-financial assets, such as property and equipment, goodwill (see Note 6: \u201cGoodwill\u201d), intangible assets, and operating lease right-of-use assets are adjusted to fair value when an impairment charge is recognized or the underlying investment is sold. Such fair value measurements, if necessary, are based predominantly on Level 3 inputs.\nNOTE 6: GOODWILL\nThe following table summarizes our goodwill activity by reportable segment for the periods presented:\nExperiences\nHotels and Other\nTotal\n(in millions)\nBalance as of December 31, 2025\n$\n309.6\n$\n414.6\n$\n724.2\nForeign currency translation adjustments\n1.4\n(0.8\n)\n0.6\nImpairment\n\u2014\n(369.5\n)\n(369.5\n)\nBalance as of September 30, 2026\n$\n311.0\n$\n44.3\n$\n355.3\nImpairment\n"}
{"page": 22, "text": "We assess goodwill, which is not amortized, for impairment annually during the fourth quarter, or more frequently, if events and circumstances indicate that it is more likely than not that an impairment has occurred. Observing persistent and challenging business trends in 2026, during the third quarter of 2026, the Company updated its long range forecast for the Hotels and Other reporting unit to reflect the accelerated decline in recent financial performance, in addition to a decline in expected future profitability of the Hotels and Other reporting unit, as compared to the outyears forecast used in its previous impairment analysis. As a result, the Company determined that an impairment triggering event had occurred as of September 30, 2026. These trends are largely due to the sustained acceleration of traffic acquisition headwinds negatively impacting this business due to the changes in the search landscape. In addition, the Company experienced a significant decline in its stock price and market capitalization during the third quarter of 2026, with its stock price declining nearly 40% from June 30, 2026 to September 30, 2026. Therefore, the Company performed an interim impairment test of its Hotels and Other reporting unit as of the last day of the third quarter, or September 30, 2026. Based on these trends, noted above, and the resulting negative impact on this reporting unit, we concluded it was more likely than not that the estimated fair value of the Hotels and Other reporting unit was less than its respective carrying value. Therefore the Company chose to proceed directly to a quantitative analysis. As a result, the Company recognized a non-cash goodwill impairment of $369.5 million in its unaudited condensed consolidated statement of operations during the three months ended September 30, 2026 for the Hotels and Other reporting unit.\nThe fair value of the Hotels and Other reporting unit using a quantitative analysis was determined with the assistance of a third party specialist using a discounted cash flow (\u201cDCF\u201d) analysis, or income approach calculation (Level 3). Fair value assessments of a reporting unit are considered a Level 3 measurement due to the significance of unobservable inputs used in their estimate. Determining fair value using a DCF analysis requires the exercise of significant judgment with respect to several items, including the amount and timing of expected future cash flows, discount rates, and the long-term growth rate used to estimate terminal value. The expected cash flows used in the DCF analysis for the Hotels and Other reporting unit were based on the Company\u2019s most recent forecast, and for years beyond the periods covered by the forecast, the Company\u2019s estimate of forecasted long-term growth rate. The discount rate used in the DCF analysis is intended to reflect the risks, which consider macroeconomic and industry specific factors inherent in the expected future cash flows of the reporting unit.\n18\n"}
{"page": 23, "text": "In conjunction with the quantitative analysis performed for the Hotels and Other reporting unit, as of September 30, 2026, the Company also performed a quantitative analysis' of its Experiences reporting unit. In addition, and separately, the Company performed a quantitative analysis for its indefinite-lived intangible assets, consisting of Tripadvisor trade name and trademarks, using the relief-from-royalty method. The Company concluded the estimated fair values of its Experiences reporting unit and indefinite-lived intangibles were substantially in excess of their carrying values. In addition, prior to performing the goodwill impairment analysis, the Company performed an impairment analysis of its reporting units' definite-lived long-lived assets (including capitalized website development) to determine if the carrying amount of its identified asset groups may not be fully recoverable, and concluded the carrying amounts were recoverable as of September 30, 2026, and no impairment was identified.\nAs of September 30, 2026, in order to corroborate its fair value conclusion, the Company combined the estimated fair values for its reporting units and performed a market capitalization reconciliation to validate the reasonableness of the implied control premium. The Company calculated the market capitalization using both the stock price as of September 30, 2026 as well as the average stock price over a reasonable period of time preceding September 30, 2026. Based on this reconciliation, the Company believes the implied control premium used to be reasonable.\nAs of September 30, 2026 and December 31, 2025, accumulated goodwill impairment losses totaled $372.6 million and $3.1 million, respectively; all related to the Hotels and Other segment.\nNOTE 7: ACCRUED EXPENSES AND OTHER CURRENT LIABILITIES\nAccrued expenses and other current liabilities consisted of the following as of the dates presented:\nSeptember 30, 2026\nDecember 31, 2025\n(in millions)\