{"page": 52, "text": "Other revenue includes click-based advertising and display-based advertising revenue from our cruise offering, as well as, Tripadvisor dining revenue. At the start of 2026, the Company no longer offered travelers access to vacation rentals, flights and rental cars offerings on its platform. Tripadvisor dining revenue includes affiliate marketing commissions earned primarily from restaurant reservation bookings on Tripadvisor-branded websites and mobile apps, fulfilled by TheFork, in addition to revenue earned from Hotels and Other\u2019s own business-to-business (\u201cB2B\u201d) restaurant offerings. Other revenue decreased $3.2 million and $9.0 million during the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, respectively, when compared to the same periods in 2025, primarily due to Tripadvisor dining revenue as we shift our strategic focus and simplify the management of our Hotels and Other segment, and to a lesser extent, the Company no longer offering vacation rentals, flights and rental cars offerings on our platform, as noted above.\n37\nExpenses\nCost of Sales\nCost of sales consists of expenses that are directly related or closely correlated to revenue generation, including direct costs, such as credit card and other booking transaction payment fees, media production costs, ad serving fees, and other revenue generating costs. In addition, cost of sales includes operating costs such as bad debt expense and non-income taxes, including sales, use, digital services, and other non-income related taxes.\nThree months ended September 30,\n% Change\nNine months ended September 30,\n% Change\n2026\n2025\n2026 vs. 2025\n2026\n2025\n2026 vs. 2025\n($ in millions)\n($ in millions)\nCost of sales\n$\n37.3\n$\n36.2\n3\n%\n$\n95.3\n$\n94.8\n1\n%\n% of revenue\n8.5\n%\n7.4\n%\n7.9\n%\n7.2\n%\nCost of sales increased $1.1 million during the three months ended September 30, 2026 when compared to the same period in 2025, primarily due to an increase in bad debt expense in Hotels and Other, partially offset by a decrease in digital service taxes. Cost of sales increased $0.5 million during the nine months ended September 30, 2026 when compared to the same period in 2025, primarily due to an increase in credit card payment processing fees as a result of revenue growth in Experiences, an increase in bad debt expense in Hotels and Other, and to a lesser extent, media production costs and other transactional costs related to generating revenue in Hotels and Other. These increases were partially offset by a decrease in digital services taxes, resulting from the repeal of enacted tax legislation in Canada during 2026 related to digital service taxes, which includes a refund of $2.0 million realized during the second quarter of 2026.\nMarketing\n"}
